Do not let the Secure Engine initialise the whole image's RAM a second time.

THIS ONE WAS MEASURED ON SILICON, not predicted.  With the call in place the
bootloader came up, ran, and printed one garbage byte.  Under the debugger:
SystemCoreClock read 4 000 000 while RCC_CFGR said the core was running off
the 80 MHz PLL, and USART1's BRR was 35 -- which is 115 200 baud at 4 MHz and
2.3 Mbaud at 80.  The UART was configured for a clock the part had left
behind twenty times over.

What __gcc_data_init does is not an SE-sized job.  ST's
2_Images_SECoreBin/STM32CubeIDE/Application/User/data_init.c zeroes
_sbss.._ebss and copies _sidata into _sdata.._edata -- the WHOLE image's
.bss and .data, as the linker symbols define them.  In ST's build those
symbols belong to the SECoreBin binary and cover the Secure Engine's RAM
alone, which is the point: SECoreBin is loaded as a blob, nothing else
initialises it, so it initialises itself on its first call.

In this rig's single ELF those same symbols cover the bootloader's RAM too,
and SE_CORE_Startup runs on the first SE call -- after SystemClock_Config
has set SystemCoreClock and after the bootloader has set up its own state.
So the first SE call silently rolled back every initialised variable in the
image and zeroed every uninitialised one.  SystemCoreClock was simply the
one that announced itself.

The call is redundant here rather than merely harmful: ST's startup_stm32l475xx.s
did exactly this copy-and-zero for the whole image before main(), the Secure
Engine's RAM included.

With the call gone, data_init.c is unreferenced and the Makefile no longer
builds it -- which also retires the patch that was needed to get it to
compile at all (it includes "stdint-gcc.h", a header this toolchain does not
ship).

Applied by the Makefile's SE_STAGE_RULE with -F0.
--- se_startup.c
+++ se_startup.c
@@ -73,7 +73,9 @@
 #elif defined (__ARMCC_VERSION)
   __arm_data_init();
 #elif defined(__GNUC__)
+#if !defined(PORT_SINGLE_IMAGE)
   __gcc_data_init();
+#endif /* !PORT_SINGLE_IMAGE */
 #endif /* __ICCARM__ */
 
   /*NOTE : other initialization may be added here */
